KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp14
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ deduced type: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ value: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The second use of vb<char> instantiates the specialization again; the new
declaration still has the placeholder type and is rejected ("symbol
'vb<char>' already declared with different type: original: signed int,
new: auto", cpp_declarator_converter.cpp combine_types).  Accepting it
there is not enough: the specialization's value then stays the
unconverted constructor-form initializer, and __CPROVER_initialize assigns
it (a type-inconsistent assignment).  With `= 7' instead of `(7)' the
program verifies.  Found while diagnosing
cpp20_range_adaptor_closure_invoked_through_invoke_impl.
